      "content": "You are very close. If we use $-5$ and $+4$, they multiply to $-20$ and add to $-1$. So we can write the equation as $(x - 5)(x + 4) = 0$. \n\nIf either of those brackets equals zero, the whole equation becomes zero. So, what would $x$ be for each bracket to make it zero?\n\n**Final Answer:** $x = 5$ or $x = -4$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Method:** Factorisation\n* **What the steps mean:**\n  * **Finding factors:** We look for two numbers that multiply to the constant ($-20$) and add to the coefficient of $x$ ($-1$).\n  * **Zero Product Property:** If two things multiply to zero, at least one of them must be zero. That is why we set $(x-5)=0$ or $(x+4)=0$.\n* **Why we use it:** It is the fastest way to solve a quadratic equation when the numbers are simple and easy to factorise.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check your factors by multiplying them back together (FOIL method) in your head or on your rough paper. If you get back to $x^2 - x - 20$, you know your answer is correct before you even move on!"
